WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:06.140
When I was in my early 30s,

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:08.860
I was an associate
at a Wall Street law firm.

00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:12.940
And I had been working 16-hour days
for seven years straight.

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:16.140
And even though,
ever since I was four years old,

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:19.380
I'd had this beautiful
and impossible dream

00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:21.220
of becoming a writer,

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:25.820
I was also pretty ambitious
and on the verge of making partner.

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:27.500
Or so I thought.

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:30.740
Because, one day, a senior partner
named Steve Shalen

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:32.980
knocked on my office door.

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:36.300
Steve was tall and distinguished
and very decent.

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:40.220
And he sat down and he reached
for the squishy stress ball on my desk,

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:43.260
and he said that I wasn't
going to be making partner after all.

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:48.460
And I remember very badly
wishing that I had a stress ball too,

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:51.340
but Steve Shalen was using mine.

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:54.940
And I remember feeling sorry
that it had fallen to Steve

00:00:54.940 --> 00:00:56.700
to be the one to tell me this news,

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:58.620
because he really was a good guy.

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:02.780
And I remember bursting into tears
right in front of him,

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:05.700
such a nonpartnerish thing to do.

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:10.180
But that very afternoon
I up and left my law firm for good.

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:13.220
And a few weeks after that
ended a seven-year relationship

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:15.020
that had always felt wrong.

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:17.380
And so, now I was in my early 30s,

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:19.460
and suddenly I had no career,

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:21.740
no love, no place to live.

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:24.460
And immediately,
I fell into a relationship

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:26.260
with a handsome musician

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:28.980
who liked to compose lyrics by day

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:32.660
and stand around a piano
with friends, singing, at night.

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:35.540
And he was the wrong guy
for various reasons,

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:37.820
but my feelings for him

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:41.020
developed into this crazy obsession,

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:45.740
the likes of which I have never
experienced before or since, thankfully.

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:50.060
No matter what I did,
I couldn't escape it.

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:52.460
Until one day, a friend said to me,

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:54.180
“You are this hooked

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:57.180
because he represents
something you're longing for.

00:01:57.220 --> 00:02:00.940
What are you longing for?"

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:04.820
And the answer came to me
with a sudden clarity.

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:06.100
It was like, of course,

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:07.740
he was the writing life

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:10.700
that I had longed for
since I was four years old.

00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:14.140
He was an emissary
from the beautiful world.

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:16.660
And just like that,
the obsession fell away,

00:02:16.660 --> 00:02:18.660
and I started writing for real.

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:24.500
Today, I want to talk with you
about a state of mind

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:27.140
that's ancient and universal

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:29.380
and enormously powerful

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:32.300
and, strangely, these days overlooked.

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:34.900
The author C.S. Lewis
called this state of mind

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:38.220
"the inconsolable longing
for we know not what."

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:41.100
And he was mesmerized by it all his life.

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:42.980
As am I.

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:45.780
When I was 22 years old
and I was still in law school,

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:47.940
some friends came by my dorm room

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:50.060
to pick me up on the way to class.

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:53.100
And I had been sitting
and listening to bittersweet music,

00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:55.860
which is something
I've been doing all my life.

00:02:57.500 --> 00:03:04.140
(Music: "Adagio in G Minor" by Albinoni)

00:03:20.740 --> 00:03:23.580
(Music ends)

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:29.420
OK, so the music is a little bit gloomy.

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:31.180
It's full of longing, right?

00:03:31.220 --> 00:03:35.300
Maybe for a lost love
that never existed in the first place,

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:36.940
but the curious thing is,

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:38.940
when I hear music like this,

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:42.060
I don't really feel sad or not very sad.

00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:46.660
What I actually feel
is something that's much closer to joy.

00:03:46.660 --> 00:03:49.860
And I could never understand why this was.

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:52.780
But meanwhile, I noticed
that my law school classmates

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:55.060
were feeling very little joy.

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:58.020
One of them asked me
why I was listening to funeral music.

00:03:58.060 --> 00:03:59.220
(Laughter)

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:02.020
And I laughed, and we went off to class.

00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:03.380
End of story.

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:07.180
Except that I thought
about that comment of his

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:09.860
for literally the next 25 years.

00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:13.420
Why do I find sad music so uplifting?

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:15.300
And what is it in our culture

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:17.940
that makes it feel
a fitting subject for a joke?

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:19.500
And why, even right this minute,

00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:23.500
do I feel an intense desire to assure you
that I love dance music too?

00:04:23.540 --> 00:04:24.860
I really do.

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:29.020
At first, these were just
interesting questions,

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:32.340
but as I looked
for the answers over the years,

00:04:32.380 --> 00:04:36.540
I started to realize
that, really, they were the questions.

00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:37.860
They were the big ones,

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:39.660
I just hadn't known it yet.

00:04:41.620 --> 00:04:43.460
So here's what I found out.

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:47.140
Lots of people feel the way I do
about sad music,

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:49.220
especially sensitive types.

00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:51.500
People play the happy songs
on their playlists

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:53.900
about 175 times on average,

00:04:53.900 --> 00:04:56.660
but they play the sad songs 800 times.

00:04:57.620 --> 00:05:00.780
And they tell researchers
that they associate sad music

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:04.060
with beauty and wonder and transcendence,

00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:06.740
the so-called sublime emotions.

00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:09.540
And you know, just think
of how many musical genres

00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:11.100
tap into sorrow, right.

00:05:11.140 --> 00:05:14.340
There’s Spanish flamenco
and Portuguese fado

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:15.700
and the Irish lament

00:05:15.700 --> 00:05:18.900
and American country music and the blues.

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:20.500
And then even lullabies,

00:05:20.540 --> 00:05:25.140
which all over the world we often use
our most heartbreaking melodies

00:05:25.180 --> 00:05:27.220
to sing our newborns to sleep.

00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:29.300
Why on Earth do we do that?

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:30.940
(Laughter)

00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:32.700
And then it's not just music, right?

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:35.300
We like rainy days and tragic drama

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:36.620
and cherry blossoms,

00:05:36.620 --> 00:05:39.340
which we celebrate
over equally lovely flowers

00:05:39.380 --> 00:05:41.380
partly because they die young.

00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:45.580
Philosophers call that
the paradox of tragedy.

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:49.020
Why do we sometimes welcome sorrow

00:05:49.060 --> 00:05:51.420
when the rest of the time
we will quite naturally

00:05:51.460 --> 00:05:53.660
do anything we can to avoid it?

00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:58.500
There's actually a scholarly debate
raging over this question,

00:05:58.540 --> 00:06:00.660
but I have come to believe

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:02.740
that really, what we are craving,

00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:04.020
at bottom,

00:06:04.060 --> 00:06:06.380
is that state of longing,

00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:09.020
that joy that's laced with sorrow.

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:10.300
Which is often triggered

00:06:10.340 --> 00:06:12.900
when we experience something so exquisite

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:16.020
that it seems to come to us
from some other world.

00:06:16.060 --> 00:06:18.660
And this is why we give
painters and rock stars

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:20.340
such exalted status.

00:06:20.380 --> 00:06:23.140
Because they're the ones
who bring us the breath of magic

00:06:23.180 --> 00:06:24.780
from that other place.

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:27.700
Except it only lasts a moment,

00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:31.260
and we really want to live there for good.

00:06:31.300 --> 00:06:34.060
Because we know that we live
in a deeply flawed world.

00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:36.420
And we have this stubborn conviction

00:06:36.460 --> 00:06:39.220
that we come from a perfect
and beautiful one

00:06:39.260 --> 00:06:41.580
that remains forever out of reach.

00:06:42.820 --> 00:06:45.300
And maybe that sounds depressing to you,

00:06:45.340 --> 00:06:48.260
but this state of mind,

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:49.540
this longing,

00:06:49.580 --> 00:06:51.020
is actually the deep source

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:54.180
of all our moonshots and our loves.

00:06:54.220 --> 00:06:55.700
It's because of longing

00:06:55.700 --> 00:06:57.300
that we play "Moonlight Sonatas"

00:06:57.340 --> 00:06:59.060
and build rockets to Mars.

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:02.660
And it's because we're all in this
same strange state of exile

00:07:02.660 --> 00:07:06.940
that we have the capacity to empathize
with each other in the first place.

00:07:06.940 --> 00:07:08.220
Our broken hearts,

00:07:08.260 --> 00:07:11.700
taught an 18-century rabbi
named Nahman of Bratslav,

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:13.780
they connect us to each other

00:07:13.780 --> 00:07:16.340
and to that other world
where the music comes form.

00:07:18.140 --> 00:07:22.660
But modern culture
is telling us a really different story.

00:07:22.660 --> 00:07:26.820
It's telling us to smile
and get over it and move on.

00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:28.140
And of course it's true,

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:31.140
positivity is very powerful as well.

00:07:31.180 --> 00:07:33.380
And it's possible to get stuck
in your longing

00:07:33.420 --> 00:07:34.740
and to never come out.

00:07:34.740 --> 00:07:36.900
So I do want to be really clear,

00:07:36.900 --> 00:07:39.260
this is not an argument for depression.

00:07:39.300 --> 00:07:40.540
And --

00:07:40.580 --> 00:07:41.860
(Laughter)

00:07:41.860 --> 00:07:43.980
If that's what you think
you're experiencing,

00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:46.500
please, please do go seek help.

00:07:46.540 --> 00:07:50.020
I'm talking about a different
state of mind that we all live in,

00:07:50.060 --> 00:07:52.500
where the light and the dark
move together.

00:07:52.540 --> 00:07:54.380
And I am saying that right now,

00:07:54.420 --> 00:07:57.060
our culture is afraid of the dark.

00:07:57.100 --> 00:07:59.180
And that all this normative sunshine

00:07:59.220 --> 00:08:02.540
can distract you
from your rightful heritage.

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:04.180
In Homer's "Odyssey,"

00:08:04.220 --> 00:08:06.100
it was homesickness,

00:08:06.140 --> 00:08:08.020
it was the longing for home

00:08:08.060 --> 00:08:12.620
that caused Odysseus to take
that epic journey in the first place.

00:08:12.620 --> 00:08:15.860
The poem starts with him
weeping on a beach.

00:08:15.860 --> 00:08:18.300
And in most every children's book
you've ever loved,

00:08:18.340 --> 00:08:20.100
the hero is an orphan first.

00:08:20.140 --> 00:08:21.380
Think of Harry Potter --

00:08:21.420 --> 00:08:24.260
broken before the story even starts.

00:08:25.300 --> 00:08:27.700
Longing is also
the beating heart, of course,

00:08:27.700 --> 00:08:29.780
of most of the world's religions.

00:08:29.780 --> 00:08:32.460
We long for Eden and Zion,

00:08:32.500 --> 00:08:34.140
and we long for the Beloved,

00:08:34.180 --> 00:08:38.020
which is the beautiful word
the Sufis have for God.

00:08:38.060 --> 00:08:43.060
So sometime after I left
my law firm and the musician,

00:08:43.100 --> 00:08:45.660
I met Ken, who would become my husband.

00:08:46.540 --> 00:08:47.820
Ken is a writer, too,

00:08:47.820 --> 00:08:50.020
and he had spent the past decade

00:08:50.060 --> 00:08:52.300
doing UN peacekeeping negotiations

00:08:52.340 --> 00:08:56.020
in Rwanda and Liberia and Somalia.

00:08:56.620 --> 00:08:58.140
And he had done that work

00:08:58.180 --> 00:09:01.700
because underneath
his very exuberant personality,

00:09:01.700 --> 00:09:05.260
he was filled with his own longing
for another world.

00:09:05.300 --> 00:09:07.340
He'd grown up haunted by the Holocaust.

00:09:07.380 --> 00:09:10.260
He used to lie awake at night
when he was 10 years old

00:09:10.300 --> 00:09:14.340
wondering would he have had the guts
to hide Anne Frank in his attic.

00:09:15.140 --> 00:09:17.940
And it turned out
that he did have the courage,

00:09:17.940 --> 00:09:21.900
even if his dreams of peace
never did come true.

00:09:21.900 --> 00:09:23.820
To this day, he keeps on his desk

00:09:23.820 --> 00:09:27.940
a photo of a pile of bones
from the Rwandan genocide.

00:09:29.580 --> 00:09:30.860
When we met,

00:09:30.860 --> 00:09:33.620
Ken was getting ready
to publish a book on his experiences,

00:09:33.620 --> 00:09:35.340
and it was getting optioned for TV.

00:09:35.380 --> 00:09:38.020
And in contrast,
I had this failed legal career

00:09:38.060 --> 00:09:40.540
and some poems that I had written.

00:09:40.580 --> 00:09:43.780
At the time, I was working
on a memoir in sonnet form,

00:09:43.780 --> 00:09:45.100
because why not.

00:09:45.140 --> 00:09:49.420
But I brought the sonnets
to our second date,

00:09:49.460 --> 00:09:51.220
and I showed them to Ken.

00:09:51.260 --> 00:09:54.020
And later that night,

00:09:54.060 --> 00:09:56.100
this is the email that he sent me.

00:09:56.140 --> 00:09:57.540
[Holy Sh*t. My favorite line:

00:09:57.580 --> 00:10:00.300
I'm Penelope's descendant/
Heiress to a life transcendent

00:10:00.340 --> 00:10:03.100
Keep writing. Drop Everything.
Write. Write woman, write.]

00:10:03.140 --> 00:10:10.140
(Laughter and applause)

00:10:11.540 --> 00:10:14.020
So Ken's exuberant faith

00:10:14.060 --> 00:10:17.020
helped to make my longing a reality.

00:10:17.060 --> 00:10:20.380
But those dreams of peace he had had,

00:10:20.420 --> 00:10:22.940
you know, we're all
still waiting for those, right?

00:10:22.940 --> 00:10:24.460
Which is the way it often goes,

00:10:24.500 --> 00:10:27.660
the beautiful world just out of reach.

00:10:27.660 --> 00:10:30.340
But while we're waiting,

00:10:30.380 --> 00:10:33.500
our broken hearts
can also help to connect us.

00:10:34.660 --> 00:10:41.380
(Music: "Adagio in G Minor" by Albinoni)

00:10:59.140 --> 00:11:01.900
(Music ends)

00:11:06.620 --> 00:11:08.180
In 1992,

00:11:08.220 --> 00:11:10.420
the city of Sarajevo was under siege.

00:11:11.460 --> 00:11:14.700
For centuries, Muslims,
Catholics and Eastern Orthodox

00:11:14.700 --> 00:11:17.900
had lived together
in this very vibrant city.

00:11:18.820 --> 00:11:21.700
And now, the streets were dead quiet.

00:11:21.700 --> 00:11:24.580
Except for the crackling sounds
of the snipers' gunshots.

00:11:25.860 --> 00:11:27.220
Until one day,

00:11:27.260 --> 00:11:29.020
when a bombed-out bakery

00:11:29.060 --> 00:11:33.060
filled with the strains
of Albinoni's "Adagio in G Minor,"

00:11:33.100 --> 00:11:35.220
which is what you've just
been listening to,

00:11:35.260 --> 00:11:39.300
thanks to the otherworldly talents
of my dear friend, Min Kym.

00:11:39.340 --> 00:11:46.180
(Applause)

00:11:48.420 --> 00:11:52.260
The Albinoni that day
was played by Vedran Smailović,

00:11:52.300 --> 00:11:54.740
the lead cellist of the Sarajevo Opera,

00:11:54.740 --> 00:11:58.220
in honor of 22 people
who had been killed the morning before,

00:11:58.260 --> 00:12:00.100
as they lined up for bread.

00:12:00.500 --> 00:12:04.060
Dressed in a formal white shirt
and black tails,

00:12:04.100 --> 00:12:05.780
as if for a night at the opera,

00:12:05.980 --> 00:12:07.980
he sat down on a white plastic chair

00:12:08.020 --> 00:12:09.340
on a mound of rubble,

00:12:09.340 --> 00:12:11.660
and he played his cello.

00:12:11.700 --> 00:12:14.460
And he did that every day for 22 days

00:12:14.500 --> 00:12:17.540
under sniper fire that never stopped him.

00:12:18.540 --> 00:12:21.820
"You ask me am I crazy to play my cello
in the middle of a war zone,"

00:12:21.860 --> 00:12:23.140
he said,

00:12:23.140 --> 00:12:26.380
"Why don't you ask them
if they're crazy for shelling Sarajevo?"

00:12:27.860 --> 00:12:29.220
A few months after that,

00:12:29.220 --> 00:12:31.740
the BBC foreign correspondent,
Allan Little, writes

00:12:31.780 --> 00:12:34.940
that he watched as a procession
of 40,000 people

00:12:34.980 --> 00:12:37.860
emerged from a nearby forest.

00:12:37.900 --> 00:12:41.940
They had been walking for 48 hours
to flee a military attack.

00:12:41.980 --> 00:12:43.860
And among them was an old man.

00:12:44.900 --> 00:12:47.020
He was dazed and exhausted.

00:12:47.860 --> 00:12:49.900
And he asked Allan Little,

00:12:49.940 --> 00:12:51.420
"Have you seen my wife,

00:12:51.420 --> 00:12:52.940
we got separated in the forest,

00:12:52.980 --> 00:12:55.340
I don't know whether she's made it."

00:12:56.420 --> 00:12:58.620
And Little said to him,

00:12:58.660 --> 00:13:02.620
"Do you mind if I ask you,
are you a Muslim or a Croat?"

00:13:03.700 --> 00:13:05.540
And the old man's answer, he says,

00:13:05.580 --> 00:13:09.020
shames him even now,
as he recalls it across the decades.

00:13:10.100 --> 00:13:12.780
"I am," said the old man,

00:13:13.820 --> 00:13:15.180
"a musician."

00:13:15.180 --> 00:13:21.500
(Music: "Adagio in G Minor")

00:13:52.140 --> 00:13:54.940
(Music ends)

00:13:59.380 --> 00:14:03.100
In Hebrew, the word
for longing, leh-heesh-toh-kek,

00:14:03.100 --> 00:14:04.780
it comes from the same word,

00:14:04.820 --> 00:14:06.100
from the same root,

00:14:06.100 --> 00:14:07.660
as the word for passion.

00:14:08.580 --> 00:14:11.660
The place you suffer
is the same exact place

00:14:11.700 --> 00:14:13.740
where you care desperately.

00:14:13.780 --> 00:14:18.220
It's the same place that inspires you
to ease someone's pain however you can.

00:14:18.220 --> 00:14:20.300
And it's the place that you vibrate

00:14:20.300 --> 00:14:23.100
with the insane beauty of this world.

00:14:23.100 --> 00:14:27.180
So, remember, there's light
and there's dark.

00:14:27.180 --> 00:14:29.300
And when the dark times come,

00:14:29.300 --> 00:14:30.900
and they will come,

00:14:30.940 --> 00:14:32.660
don't be surprised,

00:14:32.700 --> 00:14:36.380
but ask yourself:
What are you longing for?

00:14:36.380 --> 00:14:39.620
And follow your longing
where it's telling you to go.

00:14:39.660 --> 00:14:42.900
It's pointing you
in the direction of the sacred.

00:14:42.940 --> 00:14:45.580
If you don't like
that word sacred, I get it,

00:14:45.620 --> 00:14:47.540
call it the wondrous instead.

00:14:49.700 --> 00:14:53.100
You don't, you absolutely
don't have to believe

00:14:53.100 --> 00:14:54.820
in the deities of the ancient books

00:14:54.860 --> 00:14:56.980
to know that spiritual longing is real,

00:14:57.020 --> 00:15:00.340
and that humanity
is steeped in the sublime.

00:15:00.340 --> 00:15:03.500
Once you look, you will see it
everywhere around you.

00:15:03.540 --> 00:15:05.700
It's in the arch
of your best friend's eyebrow

00:15:05.700 --> 00:15:07.180
when she makes a joke.

00:15:07.180 --> 00:15:10.020
It's in the smallpox vaccines
and the Golden Gate bridges

00:15:10.060 --> 00:15:11.980
that humans somehow managed to invent

00:15:12.020 --> 00:15:14.380
against all odds.

00:15:14.380 --> 00:15:16.740
It's in the surge of love
you feel for a cellist

00:15:16.780 --> 00:15:18.500
who did a brave and miraculous thing

00:15:18.540 --> 00:15:21.060
during a misbegotten war in 1992.

00:15:24.980 --> 00:15:27.620
And it's right here in this room.

00:15:27.620 --> 00:15:30.380
Follow your longing
where it’s telling you to go,

00:15:30.380 --> 00:15:34.180
and may it carry you
straight to the beating heart

00:15:34.180 --> 00:15:36.860
of the perfect and beautiful world.

00:15:36.900 --> 00:15:38.180
Thank you so much.

00:15:38.180 --> 00:15:44.740
(Applause)

